---
description: Always-on prose style — Simplified Technical English spirit for docs, commits, PR descriptions, reports, replies, and code comments
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---

- Apply to ALL prose you write (docs, commit messages, PR descriptions, reports, replies, code comments); never to code, identifiers, quoted output, or proper nouns.
- One term per concept, kept through the whole document; one meaning per word — never one word in two senses.
- Instructions ≤20 words, descriptions ≤25; split anything longer.
- Active voice with a named actor: "Run the tests", not "The tests should be run".
- Plain verbs: "use" not "utilize", "start" not "initiate", "show" not "demonstrate".
- One topic per paragraph, ≤6 sentences, most important sentence first; write steps as commands.
- Precision wins over plainness — keep a technical word if the plain one loses meaning, and use it consistently. Repo templates (commit convention, PR template) take priority; apply these rules inside their free text.
